Below is the change description:
The original implementation inconsistently handled results from proxied MCP tools by wrapping every output in a single `TextContent` object, regardless of whether the output was actually structured JSON representing complex MCP content types (like images or resources). This caused data loss and prevented proper rendering of non-textual tool results. The change ensures that if a tool returns a JSON array of valid MCP content items, those items are correctly reconstructed as typed MCP objects rather than being treated as raw plain text.
Introduced a new parsing method, `parseContentList`, in `McpToolUtils` to handle the deserialization of MCP tool result strings into a list of specific `McpSchema.Content` types (e.g., TextContent, ImageContent, AudioContent, ResourceLink). Updated the `toSharedSyncToolSpecification` method to utilize this parser instead of hard-coding a single `TextContent` wrapper. Added a corresponding unit test class `ParseContentListTests` and expanded `ToolUtilsTests` to cover various proxy scenarios.

